    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "No, the hesitation isn't primarily about purchasing power or class itself, but rather about the *nature* of the demographic engagement. Luxury brands often rely on audiences that already value their products, whereas drill or rap-driven scenes frequently include a massive younger, less financially embedded consumer base that views these genres more as a lifestyle or status symbol than high consumption. While some wealthy individuals enjoy reggaeton and some drill fans have disposable income, the cultural output of these scenes is deeply geared toward accessibility, viral reach, and rugged individualism, which can feel misaligned with the luxury narrative of exclusivity, legacy, and quiet confidence. However, this is shifting rapidly, as ultra-high-net-worth individuals increasingly embrace street culture for its authenticity, forcing luxury brands to navigate a fine line between appropriation and genuine partnership to avoid seeming out of touch without losing their core legacy.",
